WEBVTT

00:00:01.679 --> 00:00:05.799
Hey, this is Alec Lev, the producer of How We Made Your Mother.
大家好，我是《How We Made Your Mother》的制作人 Alec Lev。

00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:10.359
We began our 14th episode this season with a voice note from Michaela.
本季第14集，我们以 Michaela 发来的一条语音留言作为开场。

00:00:10.359 --> 00:00:14.439
That note ended with this very important question she had for her girlfriend.
那条留言的结尾，是她向女友提出的一个非常重要的问题。

00:00:14.439 --> 00:00:24.320
Yeah, and I wanted to say to Carmina, you're good looking, you're funny, you're smart, now would you freaking marry me already?
“是啊，我想对 Carmina 说，你长得好看，又幽默又聪明，你到底愿不愿意嫁给我？”

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:30.679
A few weeks later, I got a follow up note from Michaela, which I surprised Josh and Craig with just the other day.
几周后，我收到了 Michaela 的后续留言，前几天我刚给 Josh 和 Craig 听了，给了他们一个惊喜。

00:00:30.679 --> 00:00:33.915
Hi guys, it's Michaela and Carmina here.
“嘿大家，我是 Michaela 和 Carmina。”

00:00:33.915 --> 00:00:37.880
We just wanted to fill you in about our proposal.
“我们只是想告诉你们我们的求婚结果。”

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.577
Oh my god, oh my god, oh my god.
“天哪，天哪，天哪。”

00:00:40.577 --> 00:00:42.600
Yes, very, very engaged.
“是的，我们订婚了，非常非常开心。”

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.079
Making this such a legendary experience.
“这让整个经历变得如此传奇。”

00:00:46.039 --> 00:00:54.840
Hemium has helped me throughout all of my chapters of my young adulthood, navigating my career, friendship, heart aches, and my love life.
“《老爸老妈浪漫史》陪伴我度过了青年时期的每一个阶段，指引着我的职业生涯、友谊、心碎时刻以及爱情生活。”

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:05.331
One of my favorite quotes from Him Yim was should we hold out for the person who doesn't just tolerate our little quirks but actually kind of likes them.
“我最喜欢的一句台词是：我们是否应该等待那个不仅能包容我们的小怪癖，而且实际上还挺喜欢它们的人？”

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:10.062
And I am so lucky to have a person who loves all of my little quirks.
“我很幸运，遇到了一个爱我所有小怪癖的人。”

00:01:10.062 --> 00:01:16.920
I am forever grateful for Mikayla, my chosen family, and all of you who created this wonderful show.
“我永远感激 Michaela，感激我选择的家人，也感激你们所有创作了这部精彩剧集的人。”

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:17.463
Oh man.
“天哪。”

00:01:17.463 --> 00:01:23.753
Thank you guys for being such an amazing part of my big grand ten Mosvie Gesture.
“谢谢你们成为我这场盛大泰德·莫斯比式告白中如此精彩的一部分。”

00:01:23.753 --> 00:01:36.798
It's been amazing to be able to propose like this and I'm forever grateful that we'll have this recording and we can look back on this and feel the love all over again.
“能以这种方式求婚真是太棒了，我永远感激我们留下了这段录音，以后回看时能再次感受到这份爱。”

00:01:36.798 --> 00:01:40.758
I really appreciate you guys and thank you so much.
“我真的很感激你们，非常感谢。”

00:01:40.758 --> 00:01:43.010
We'll see you at the wedding.
“婚礼上见。”

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:44.485
Wow, it's the best.
“哇，太棒了。”

00:01:44.485 --> 00:01:47.358
Oh my congratulations to both of you.
“噢，恭喜你们两位。”

00:01:47.358 --> 00:01:55.900
That's amazing Ted Mosvie would be so proud of you and thank you for letting us be part of your grand gesture.
“太神奇了，泰德·莫斯比一定会为你们感到骄傲的，谢谢你们让我们参与到你们的盛大告白中。”

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.620
We did not know Alec was gonna play that for us.
“我们完全不知道 Alec 会给我们放这段录音。”

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.620
That was our first time hearing it.
“这是我们第一次听到。”

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.260
And it was a total, it's a wonderful surprise.
“这真是一个非常美妙的惊喜。”

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:06.700
And if you haven't heard a follow-up, we, one can assume it just, it didn't go well.
“如果你们还没听到后续，大家可能会以为，这事儿没成。”

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:07.540
Yeah.
“是啊。”

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:08.819
It went to rad disastrously.
“结果简直是一场灾难。”

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:10.780
We were getting worried because we weren't hearing.
“我们当时很担心，因为一直没收到消息。”

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.819
Yeah, and you know, the truth be said, like, Mosby wasn't bad in a thousand.
“是啊，说实话，莫斯比的成功率可没那么高。”

00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:17.300
No, no, no, really low batting average.
“没错，没错，胜率真的很低。”

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.120
Not all of him.
“并不是每一段感情都像他那样。”

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.060
So, but he landed on his feet.
“但他最终还是站稳了脚跟。”

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.699
But you guys definitely did too.
“你们俩也绝对做到了。”

00:02:22.699 --> 00:02:27.020
and we're thrilled to have played a small part in your story.
“我们很高兴能在你们的故事中扮演一个小角色。”

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.860
So thank you so much for including us.
“非常感谢你们把我们包含在内。”

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:29.860
The invitation's binding.
“邀请函可是有约束力的。”

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:30.699
We're coming, right, Josh?
“我们要去参加，对吧，Josh？”

00:02:30.699 --> 00:02:31.699
We're going to the swimming pool.
“我们要去游泳池。”

00:02:31.699 --> 00:02:33.300
We're the wedding band.
“我们是婚礼乐队。”

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:39.830
I'm a low EI will be soon.
“我很快就会成为一名低音吉他手。”

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:45.639
You know York City when I see you.
“我一眼就能认出纽约市。”

00:02:45.639 --> 00:02:53.560
Please permit me to tell you everything in New York City.
“请允许我告诉你们关于纽约市的一切。”

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.240
Craig, can you believe it?
“Craig，你敢相信吗？”

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.479
I can't.
“我不敢。”

00:02:57.479 --> 00:02:58.439
Can you believe it?
“你敢相信吗？”

00:02:58.439 --> 00:02:59.360
I can't believe it.
“我简直不敢相信。”

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.400
It's gone by too fast.
“时间过得太快了。”

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:14.360
It comes to the end of season two of How We Made Your Mother, which is also the end of season two of How I Met Your Mother, which is the show that we're re-watching that we made together all those many years ago.
“《How We Made Your Mother》第二季即将结束，这也意味着《老爸老妈浪漫史》第二季的结束，也就是我们多年前共同制作、现在正在重温的那部剧。”

00:03:15.439 --> 00:03:26.680
And today, yeah, we're talking about episode 22, something blue, a fantastic season finale written by, I believe, you and Carter Bayes.
“今天，我们要聊的是第22集《Something Blue》，这是一集精彩的季终集，我想是由你和 Carter Bayes 写的。”

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:27.597
that is correct.
“没错。”

00:03:27.597 --> 00:03:28.687
It was our episode.
“那是我们写的。”

00:03:28.687 --> 00:03:30.178
I really love this finale.
“我真的很喜欢这一集季终集。”

00:03:30.178 --> 00:03:33.504
This is one of my favorite season finales that we've done.
“这是我最喜欢的季终集之一。”

00:03:33.504 --> 00:03:35.798
I also love the season one finale a lot.
“我也非常喜欢第一季的季终集。”

00:03:35.798 --> 00:03:40.100
These two might be on my top five, top 10 kind of list of the whole series.
“这两集可能都在我整个系列的前五或前十名单里。”

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.075
I love these two.
“我爱死这两集了。”

00:03:41.075 --> 00:03:43.139
But today we'll talk about this one.
“但今天我们先聊这一集。”

00:03:43.139 --> 00:03:43.639
Okay.
“好的。”

00:03:43.639 --> 00:03:45.569
I'll tell us when this episode aired.
“我来说说这一集是什么时候播出的。”

00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:49.689
This episode aired one week after the last one which gets us to May 14th, 2007.
“这一集是在上一集播出的一周后播出的，也就是2007年5月14日。”

00:03:49.689 --> 00:03:50.189
Wow.
“哇。”

00:03:50.189 --> 00:03:50.815
Nice sweeps.
“不错的收视扫荡期。”

00:03:50.815 --> 00:03:51.388
It's weird.
“真奇怪。”

00:03:51.388 --> 00:03:54.048
Usually we'd go a little deeper into May than that.
“通常我们会在五月更深入一点。”

00:03:54.048 --> 00:03:58.585
I wonder, I'm trying to recreate my mind why we didn't get to late May with our finale.
“我在想，我试图在脑海中重现为什么我们的季终集没有排到五月下旬。”

00:03:58.585 --> 00:04:04.322
May when we started having 24 episode seasons, which started in season three, we got a little deeper into May.
“当我们开始制作24集的季度时，也就是从第三季开始，我们确实更深入五月了。”

00:04:04.322 --> 00:04:05.678
Anyway, did we get a seat?
“总之，我们当时拿到续订了吗？”

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:11.727
Did we get a two season pick up in episode season two or was that episode season three, we got a two season pick up?
“我们是在第二季拿到了两季续订，还是在第三季才拿到的？”

00:04:11.727 --> 00:04:12.770
I think that was it.
“我想应该是那时候。”

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:17.672
I think at the end of season three, I think I would remember that because that was a big deal.
“我觉得是在第三季结束时，我应该记得，因为那是一件大事。”

00:04:17.672 --> 00:04:23.409
I think it was the, because we were because, yeah, I think it was that because we really started to grow then.
“我想是因为……是的，我想是因为那时候我们真的开始成长了。”

00:04:23.409 --> 00:04:28.050
I think we got on to the show got into Netflix, the numbers are going to work on Netflix.
“我想是因为剧集上了 Netflix，数据在 Netflix 上表现不错。”

00:04:27.519 --> 00:04:30.279
Yeah, so maybe somewhere around there, anyway.
“是的，所以大概是那个时候，总之。”

00:04:30.279 --> 00:04:31.279
Yeah.
“是的。”

00:04:31.279 --> 00:04:34.519
That's a good feeling for a young actor, the two-season pickup.
“对于年轻演员来说，两季续订是一种很好的感觉。”

00:04:34.519 --> 00:04:35.519
Yeah.
“是的。”

00:04:35.519 --> 00:04:37.480
You can go buy a car or whatever.
“你可以去买辆车什么的。”

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.519
You can do something, you'll get a little big when you get that.
“你可以做点什么，拿到续订时你会觉得有点膨胀。”

00:04:40.519 --> 00:04:53.759
You've got to, you've got some, well, it's also just nice to know, I know what the next two years are going to generally look like, I even generally look like, I know when I'll have some time off, I can either travel or do another job.
“你得……好吧，知道接下来的两年大致会是什么样子也很好，我知道什么时候有空，可以去旅行或做别的工作。”

00:04:53.759 --> 00:05:01.339
So things started to get, you know, stay, we were getting, I suppose, a little more out of the woods.
“所以事情开始变得……你知道，稳定下来了，我想我们稍微走出困境了。”

00:05:01.339 --> 00:05:09.660
Like it felt, it felt like we, at the very least there was a steady growth of our members were, were not going down.
“感觉至少我们的收视率在稳步增长，没有下降。”

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.339
They were, no, no, they were, they were doing, we were doing well at this point.
“不，不，它们在增长，那时候我们表现得很好。”

00:05:12.339 --> 00:05:13.339
It wasn't huge.
“虽然不是特别火。”

00:05:13.339 --> 00:05:14.339
It got bigger.
“后来变得更火了。”

00:05:14.339 --> 00:05:19.699
We sort of grew past this, but I remember feeling, I felt very, I felt like we're in a good place.
“我们算是超越了那个阶段，但我记得当时的感觉，我觉得我们处于一个很好的位置。”

00:05:19.699 --> 00:05:23.740
And then re-watching this episode last night, I felt calm.
“昨晚重看这一集时，我感到很平静。”

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:24.899
I felt the sense of calm.
“我感受到了一种平静。”

00:05:24.899 --> 00:05:27.740
I remembered the feeling like we're in good shape here.
“我记得当时的感觉，我们状态很好。”

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:29.699
This is creatively good, and people are watching.
“创作上很棒，而且有人在看。”

00:05:29.699 --> 00:05:37.459
But isn't it weird, back in the day, if all your friends were telling you, you've got to watch this show, how I met your mother, well into season two.
“但奇怪的是，在过去，如果所有的朋友都告诉你，你一定要看《老爸老妈浪漫史》，而剧集已经播到第二季了。”

00:05:37.459 --> 00:05:40.180
You could just jump in and start watching it, and I suppose you would.
“你只能中途加入开始看，我想你也会这么做。”

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:45.500
But you're like, how would you go, you'd have to wait until re-runs to catch up, right?
“但你会想，你怎么追剧呢？你得等重播才能补上，对吧？”

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:49.779
This makes it sound very old, but yes, you had, there was no way to catch up.
“这听起来很老派，但确实，当时没法补剧。”

00:05:49.779 --> 00:05:51.939
You went to someone's house who t-vowed them all?
“你得去别人家看他们录下来的电视节目？”

00:05:51.939 --> 00:05:53.279
Yeah.
“是的。”

00:05:53.279 --> 00:05:57.180
Or maybe you could, I guess you could buy, could you buy them on iTunes yet?
“或者也许你可以……我想当时能在 iTunes 上买吗？”

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:59.540
Like you could buy episodes in 2007?
“2007年能买到剧集吗？”

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:00.699
No, I don't think you could.
“不，我觉得不行。”

00:06:00.699 --> 00:06:01.540
I don't think you could.
“我觉得不行。”

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:02.379
DVDs.
DVD。
DVD。

00:06:02.379 --> 00:06:03.379
You could buy, do you just buy the DVD?
你可以买，你是只买DVD吗？

00:06:03.379 --> 00:06:06.980
If you bought the DVD, you could catch up, but yeah, we were not, yeah.
如果你买了DVD，你就能补上进度，但确实，我们当时没有，是的。

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:07.819
We were not a big hit.
我们当时并不是大热门。

00:06:07.819 --> 00:06:11.860
Well, you know, one of the things that I, we've talked about this a little bit, but I kind of wanted to bring it up again.
嗯，你知道，我们之前稍微聊过这个，但我还是想再提一下。

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:29.939
There's a scene, I think it's in showdown, or there's a, I mean, there seems throughout this, But when Barney burst in with his entrance, practicing his entrances to the prices, right, Robin and Ted are sitting on the couch and they're reading the newspaper, the actual newspaper.
有一个场景，我想是在《决战时刻》里，或者说，我是说，整部剧里似乎都有，但当巴尼冲进来，练习他那些出场方式时，对吧，罗宾和泰德坐在沙发上读报纸，真正的报纸。

00:06:29.939 --> 00:06:31.939
A hard copy of the newspaper.
纸质版的报纸。

00:06:31.939 --> 00:06:32.779
Yeah.
是的。

00:06:32.779 --> 00:06:37.779
Now, if you were doing a show now, like at least one of them would be on their phones, right?
现在，如果你现在拍一部剧，至少其中一个人会拿着手机，对吧？

00:06:37.779 --> 00:06:41.420
Like that, inarguably, like someone would be on the phone if not both of them on the phone.
就像那样，毫无疑问，如果不是两个人都在看手机，至少也会有一个人在看。

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:43.379
Yeah, especially if they're in their 20s, right?
是的，尤其是如果他们才20多岁的话，对吧？

00:06:43.379 --> 00:06:50.300
Or you would talk about it, like, look at this guy, he still gets a newspaper, it's 2026, why the fuck does he have a newspaper?
或者你会谈论它，比如，看这家伙，他还在看报纸，现在是2026年了，他妈的为什么还要看报纸？

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.899
Or you'd be sharing, you know, videos or whatever.
或者你会分享视频之类的东西。

00:06:53.899 --> 00:07:11.240
But I think some of the Gen Z and teenage kind of discovery of this show, there is a longing for like analog, like they realize that like 90s childhoods, 80s childhoods, pre-2007 life was not so digitally bound.
但我认为Z世代和青少年对这部剧的某种发现，是因为他们渴望那种模拟时代，他们意识到90年代、80年代的童年，2007年之前的生活并没有被数字设备完全束缚。

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:13.600
And I really think people hunger for it.
我真的认为人们渴望那种生活。

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:17.600
And there's something calming about seeing a non-digital world.
看到一个非数字化的世界有一种让人平静的力量。

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:26.399
And I just heard James Tallarico, who's a state senator from Texas who's running for, to send it in Texas.
我刚听了詹姆斯·塔拉里科（James Tallarico）的节目，他是德克萨斯州的一名州参议员，正在竞选德州的参议员。

00:07:26.399 --> 00:07:27.560
I really admire this guy.
我真的很钦佩这个人。

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:30.439
I think he's really smart, very thoughtful.
我觉得他非常聪明，非常有思想。

00:07:30.439 --> 00:07:38.560
He was on Ezra Klein and he said the biggest competition to the tech platforms and to AI is human connection.
他上了埃兹拉·克莱恩（Ezra Klein）的节目，他说科技平台和人工智能最大的竞争对手是人与人之间的联系。

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:40.360
It's actual in person.
是真正的面对面交流。

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:41.199
That's the enemy.
那是敌人。

00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:42.360
That's what they're fighting against.
那是他们正在对抗的东西。

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:46.480
Yeah, yeah, they're trying to keep us on the screen.
是的，是的，他们试图把我们困在屏幕前。

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:51.519
And the revolution is to be off them and to be in the world and to be face to face with people.
而革命就是离开屏幕，走进现实世界，与人面对面交流。

00:07:51.519 --> 00:07:54.920
It doesn't Netflix say our biggest competitor is sleep.
Netflix不是说他们最大的竞争对手是睡眠吗？

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:55.759
Yes.
是的。

00:07:55.759 --> 00:07:57.199
Which is also disturbing.
这也挺令人不安的。

00:07:57.199 --> 00:07:58.199
Yes.
是的。

00:07:58.199 --> 00:07:59.040
Yeah.
是的。

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:09.959
So I think there's something, part of the magic is in some of these older shows is like a longing for a time where there wasn't that battle, that like epic battle between the screen and real life.
所以我认为，这些老剧之所以有魔力，部分原因在于人们渴望那个没有这种战斗的时代，那种屏幕与现实生活之间史诗般的战斗。

00:08:09.959 --> 00:08:12.199
Yeah, yeah, I absolutely think that.
是的，是的，我绝对这么认为。

00:08:12.199 --> 00:08:21.639
And I mean, it makes the sound a little old, but it looked to this episode from 2007, it's 19 years ago, it's part of it.
我是说，这听起来有点老派，但看看这一集，2007年的，那是19年前了，这也是它的一部分。

00:08:21.639 --> 00:08:22.480
